Description:
Drag, also known as air resistance, is the force that air exerts on aircraft which slows them down. Follow a trail around our collections to see how this has shaped aeroplanes before learning more through science demonstrations with our facilitators. Then build your own model parachutist. Presented by National Museums Scotland.

	&nbsp;

	Please note: This is a drop-in event which runs from 12pm - 4pm.&nbsp;

	The event duration above is an estimate as you can drop-in to start and leave the event at anytime during this period when it is convenient for you to do so.

An astonishing tale of power and sexual addiction.
Mr Devereaux is a powerful man who controls billions of dollars every day.  A man who controls the economic fate of nations.  A man who dreams of saving the world and who cannot save himself.  A terrified man. A lost man.  Watch him fall. [18]
&nbsp;

In 1964, as a young lecturer at The University of Edinburgh, Peter Higgs hypothesised the existence of a special particle - a boson that gives mass to everything in the universe. Fast-forward nearly 50 years and researchers at the European Organisation for Nuclear Research (CERN) recently confirmed the existence of this particle, named the Higgs Boson, leading to them and Peter being jointly awarded the Edinburgh Medal before Peter went on to receive the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2013. Join Prof Higgs in conversation with fellow particle physicist and science writer Prof Frank Close as he discusses his life&#39;s work.

	&nbsp;Please note: The special student ticket offer on this event is now closed.&nbsp;

	&nbsp;

	Speaker Information

	Prof Peter Higgs

	Peter Higgs, a particle-physics theorist at the University of Edinburgh, showed in 1964 how to give mass to fundamental particles that would otherwise remain massless. Peter&rsquo;s work led to the so-called Standard Model of elementary particle interactions which has been confirmed by decades of work at particle physics laboratories around the world, but it also predicted the existence of a mysterious new particle that became known as the Higgs boson. The Large Hadron Collider (LHC) has been built to search for the elusive boson, a discovery that lead to Peter being awarded the Nobel Prize for Physics.

	Prof Frank Close

	Frank Close is professor of theoretical physics at the University of Oxford, and Fellow of Exeter College, Oxford. He is a high energy particle physicist, and has published some 200 research papers. Frank is the author of several popular books on physics, including Antimatter, Neutrino and Nothing - A Very Short Introduction.&nbsp;

Diabetes and other chronic conditions are on the rise, as is our awareness of them, and this poses a human, social and economic challenge for Scotland. Our panel will debate the effectiveness of existing healthcare services for these conditions and explore what can be done to improve them, drawing from our understanding of anthropology, design and human behaviour. Join our experts - Dr Alexandra Greene, George Valiotis and Dr Margaret Whoriskeyas they discuss how best to address these challenges and provide optimum experiences and outcomes for all, while ensuring that you, the individual, is at the centre of any solutions.

	To access this event please use the National Museum of Scotland&rsquo;s rear entrance on Lothian Street.

	Speaker Information:

	Dr Alexandra Greene is Senior Research Fellow in Medical Anthropology and Director of the NEXUS programme at the University of Dundee.&nbsp; The NEXUS Programme is an interdisciplinary research forum that has established an agenda of novel research in the field of medical anthropology. Nexus encompasses a range of topics relating to chronic illness, lifestyles, identity, race, adherence and concordance with therapy.&nbsp; Dr Greene augments the clinical research of Nexus through her research on the cultural impact on health and chronic illness of the young.&nbsp;

	George Valiotis is the Chief Executive of HIV Scotland and has extensive experience in policy and capacity building across Europe and Australasia in HIV, diabetes and family mediation amongst others.&nbsp; George chairs the HIV Voluntary Sector Network, the Third Sector Subgroup for the Sexual Health and Blood Borne Virus Framework, and is a member of the National Sexual Health, HIV and Hepatitis Advisory Group.&nbsp;

	Dr Margaret Whoriskey is the Director of the Joint Improvement Team (JIT), a uniquely-positioned body that is in partnership with the Scottish Government, NHSScotland, COSLA (Convention of Scottish Local Authorities) and the Third, Independent and Housing Sectors.&nbsp; The JIT seeks to aid the ongoing improvement of local health, housing and social care partnerships across Scotland.&nbsp; Dr Whoriskey and her team are aiming to accelerate the pace of improvement towards the Scottish Government&rsquo;s vision for 2020, whereby each of us is able to lead a longer, healthier life at home or in our own choice of setting in an integrated health and social care environment. &nbsp;

